# A shell script must reach a POSIX host with LF endings, whatever the checkout does.
#
# Found the expensive way: `benchmarks/harness/run-programme.sh` ran fine when it was first written on this
# machine, and then failed with `set: Illegal option -` the moment a `git checkout` re-materialised it. The
# working tree here is CRLF (core.autocrlf), a `sh` on Linux reads `set -e\r` as an option called `-\r`, and
# the script dies on its first line — inside a container, where the error is one line with no context.
#
# WHO THIS ACTUALLY AFFECTS, because the first version of this comment overstated it. The committed bytes are
# LF and always have been, so a Linux or macOS checkout is unaffected and no deployment ever saw this. It
# reaches exactly one person: a contributor on Windows, whose checkout converts to CRLF, at the moment a POSIX
# shell runs that working tree — a container bind-mounting it, or WSL. That is a narrow case and it is still
# worth pinning, because it is invisible until it happens and the error names nothing useful.
*.sh text eol=lf
